 ],
 "literal": "In the broad-based mortar, wide-mouthed, singing the vaḷḷai song / stranger people, what they speak, they will speak. / Why indeed should it perish, to this foolish town? / Poṟaiyaṉ of great ornaments, on his fear-filled Kolli hills, / the dark-eyed deity, on the western hill, painted / the beautiful-natured statue, resembling this / gentle-natured young girl, if she sings and pounds grain!",
 "translation": "Strangers will gossip about whatever they want to say. Why should this foolish town ruin itself? If this gentle young girl—who resembles the beautiful statue painted on the western slopes of the fear-inspiring Kolli hills of Poṟaiyaṉ of great ornaments by a dark-eyed deity—sings the vaḷḷai song while pounding grain in the wide-mouthed, broad-based mortar, what is there to gossip about?",
 "modern": "அகன்ற அடியையும் அகன்ற வாயையும் உடைய உரலில் நெல் குத்தும்போது பாடும் வள்ளைப் பாட்டைப் பாடி, இப்பெண் நெல் குற்றினால், அயலார் பலவாறு அலர் தூற்றுவர். பெரும் அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த பொறையனது, அச்சம் தரும் கொல்லி மலையின் மேற்குப் பக்கத்தில் கரிய கண்களை உடைய தெய்வம் எழுதிய நல்ல இயல்புடைய பாவை போன்றவள் இந்த மெல்லிய இயல்புடைய இளமகள். இவள் பாடி நெல் குற்றினால், இந்த அறிவற்ற ஊர் ஏன் வீணாக அலர் பேசி அழிய வேண்டும்?",
 "explanation": "In this Marutam poem, the heroine's friend (tōḻi) speaks within the hearing of the hero, addressing the gossip spreading in the town. The heroine is pounding grain in a wide-mouthed mortar while singing the traditional vaḷḷai song. Her breathtaking beauty is compared to the famous Kolli Pāvai, a divine statue painted by a dark-eyed deity on the western slopes of the Kolli hills belonging to the Cēra king Poṟaiyaṉ. The friend mocks the foolish townspeople who start gossiping and ruining the town's peace just because this gentle, statue-like girl is singing while doing her chores. Through this comparison, the friend highlights the heroine's extraordinary, divine beauty and subtly urges the hero to marry her quickly to put an end to the public gossip.",
